To apply a Gaussian filter before using the Sobel operator for edge detection, you need to add a few lines of code to your existing MATLAB script. Here's the modified code snippet:

```MATLAB
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
filter_size = 5; % Size of Gaussian kernel (odd number)
sigma = 1.5; % Standard deviation of Gaussian distribution
gaussian_kernel = fspecial('gaussian', filter_size, sigma);
filtered_image = filter2(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'replicate');

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified version, we first define a Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial()` function. We specify the filter size (an odd number, as it should be) and standard deviation (sigma). Then, we apply the Gaussian filter to the grayscale image using the `filter2()` function. Finally, we proceed with the Sobel edge detection steps as before.

Note that the larger the filter size or standard deviation, the more smoothing the Gaussian filter will perform. However, increasing these values may also cause some loss of detail in the final edge image. It's essential to find the right balance between smoothing and preserving details

