The error you're encountering is due to an input mismatch exception being thrown by the Scanner class in Java. This usually happens when the user enters a value that doesn't match the expected data type. In your case, the issue is occurring when trying to read an integer using the nextInt() method.

To resolve this problem, you can add a try-catch block around the code that reads the input and handle the InputMismatchException appropriately. Here's an example of how you can modify your code:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter the first integer: ");
            int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

            System.out.print("Enter the second integer: ");
            int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

            // Use the retrieved integers for further processing
            int sum = num1 + num2;
            System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is " + sum);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid integer.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining input on the line
        }
    }
}
```

In this modified code, we wrap the input reading and processing code in a try-catch block. If an InputMismatchException is thrown, we catch it and display an error message. We then consume any remaining input on the line using the nextLine() method to prevent any further errors.

With these changes, your program should now be able to handle invalid input and continue executing without crashing.

