To create this effect in React Native, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a ScrollView component that wraps your content.
2. Define a state variable to keep track of the focused section.
3. Create a function to handle the scroll event and check if the user is trying to scroll to an unfocused section. If they are, prevent the scroll and animate the scroll back to the focused section.
4. Add a button or other UI element to allow the user to manually focus on a different section.
5. In the state management, update the focused section when the user completes a task in the current section.
6. Add a bounce animation to the scroll view to simulate the feeling of hitting a barrier when trying to scroll past the last focused section.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { ScrollView, View, Text, Button } from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const [focusedSection, setFocusedSection] = useState(0);

  const handleScroll = (event) => {
    const offset = event.nativeEvent.contentOffset.y;
    if (offset > (focusedSection + 1) * 60) {
      // Prevent the scroll and animate the scroll back to the focused section
      event.preventDefault();
      Animated.timing(event.nativeEvent.layoutMeasurement.height, {
        toValue: focusedSection * 60,
        duration: 300,
        useNativeDriver: true,
      }).start();
    }
  };

  const handleFocusSection = (section) => {
    setFocusedSection(section);
  };

  return (
    <ScrollView onScroll={handleScroll}>
      <View style={{ flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' }}>
        <Text style={{ fontSize: 24 }}>Section {focusedSection + 1}</Text>
        <Button title="Focus on Section 2" onPress={() => handleFocusSection(1)} />
        <Button title="Focus on Section 3" onPress={() => handleFocusSection(2)} />
      </View>

