Here's an R code snippet to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
can_convert_to_numeric <- function(x) {
  tryCatch(
    {
      sapply(!is.na(x), as.numeric) == x
    },
    error = function(e) FALSE
  )
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", NA, "5")
if (can_convert_to_numeric(x)) {
  print("All non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and calls `next()` if it's equal to 'true', but returns `false` if it's equal to 'false':

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = req.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    }

    return false;
  }
}
```

To use this decorator, you can add it to your controller or route like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvGuard } from './env.guard';

@Controller('api')
export class MyController {
  @Get()
  @EnvGuard()
  getData(): any {
    // Your code here
  }
}
```

This will ensure that the `getData` method is only called when the NODE_ENV variable is set to 'true'. If it's set to 'false', the request will be rejected with a status code of 403 (Forbidden).

