To make the generated planets less close together, you can adjust the minimum distance between them in the `generateRandomPlanets` function. You can also add a check to ensure that no two planets are created within the same sphere of influence. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance, sphereInfluenceRadius) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet or within the same sphere of influence
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance ||
          Math.sqrt(planet.position.reduce((a, b) => a + b ** 2, 0)) < sphereInfluenceRadius;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to pass the desired minimum distance and sphere influence radius values when calling this function. Adjust these values to achieve the desired distribution of planets.

To create a React utility that removes widows, you'll need to use the `React` library and implement the logic to detect and remove widows. Here's a simple example:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [processedText, setProcessedText] = useState(null);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = text.split(' ');
    const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;

    for (let i = lastWordIndex - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const word = words[i];
      const nextWord = words[i + 1];

      if (word.endsWith('.') && nextWord.startsWith(word)) {
        words.splice(i, 1);
      }
    }

    setProcessedText(words.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      {processedText && <p>{processedText}</p>}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes a `text` prop, which is the input text that you want to remove widows from. When you click the "Remove Widows" button, the component will process the text and update the `processedText` state with the modified text. The modified text will be displayed below the button.

The logic used in this component is simple: it checks for consecutive words that end with a period and start with the same word. If such a pair is found, it removes the second word from the text. This helps to prevent widows, which are sentences or phrases that are broken up by a line break or other formatting element.

Note that this is a basic implementation and may not work perfectly for all types of text. You can improve the logic by adding more complex rules or using a third-party library that specializes in text processing.

